add("Kb80C01C1", "critic", f"""Attack C1. Your job is to kill the C1 claim, not to help it. Read {L}/plans/round-80.md § C1 and the E-seat outputs as they land. Attack surfaces: (1) the erratum — is `LINE_CAP` 40→60 with two new pins a guard weakening that lets a laundering world through? Construct THREE new shortcut worlds NOT in the plan's W0–W7 (e.g. an instrument edit INSIDE the size cap that weakens a check but keeps the pinned blob? impossible under pins — then find what the pins do NOT cover: the floors registry, the baseline json, floors tables inside check_assertion_floors.py (a pinned instrument!), the SKILL.md rows, the symlink target); score each on the amended counter — any reaching 6 of 6 is a kill; (2) the grammar class — does classifying `owner/repo@<hex>` as INFO weaken honest detection (construct a world where a REAL lane dead commit is hidden in @sha clothing — e.g. `kotoba-shokunin/kotoba-shokunin@<dead-lane-sha>` — and argue whether the classification lets it pass; check what the gates actually do with it); (3) the symlink — a world where the symlink points at a WRONG file with matching sha? (infeasible — but a dangling-link world outside the fleet, and a world where the fleet file is REPLACED by a marker, must not green the gate); (4) the predicate's two forms — run the absolute form at the stack world and confirm identical output. Publish every world's command, output and own exit. Verdict: KILLED or SURVIVED with the exact residual risk.""")

add("Kb80C02C2", "critic", f"""Attack C2. Kill it if you can. Surfaces: (1) vacuity — the exit predicate is form-level and the generator reaches 6/6: does the claim then assert anything a token-dump world with correct snapshots cannot? (C4 IS the claimed work — argue whether 'documented' has any meaning beyond 'files exist with matching hashes'; is that a depth move or a coverage move wearing depth's clothes? name the axis honestly); (2) the exploratory rider — reviewers of record are the future closure: is that a named non-author reviewer per AXES.md, or a hope? what happens to the claim if the closure declines to review semantics? (3) the six rows — the quotes/pins were r79's; this round only re-fetched: does 'opened this round' hold when the counter checks `opened == verified-at == 2026-10-27` (same date, but whose fetch? does the counter bind the FETCH RECORD bytes or just the date string?); (4) the Q70 banner/subtitle conjunct for the HTML snapshot (S229) — is the located quote outside the subtitle block by the counter's own text-view rule? Verify mechanically. Publish {W}/w1/Kb80C02C2.md with verdict KILLED or SURVIVED + residuals.""")

add("Kb80C03Residual", "critic", f"""Attack the residuals and the no-harm claim. (1) What does the round HARM? The erratum raises the instrument budget — every future round inherits cap 60: enumerate what a future laundering world can do with 60 lines that it could not with 40 (be concrete: 22 spent + 38 headroom; which single-instrument edits become possible and do the pins still catch them?); is the harm priced anywhere in the plan? (2) The baseline refresh re-records observed values (tracked_files, report_total, commit_total) — does the refresh script verify the observed values against an independent source or merely transcribe the gate's own output (a self-sealing baseline)? (3) The S232+ rejection rows grow the registry — W04 duplicates: does the census still exit 0 and what do the new warnings say? (4) The round leaves `check_report_pointers.py` and `check_sibling_census.py` runs to the L1 — what if they red at the final tip (name the exact fallback the plan prescribes — is there one?). Publish {W}/w1/Kb80C03Residual.md.""")

add("Kb80C04Honesty", "critic", f"""Honest-stop audit. Read {L}/plans/round-80.md end to end and enumerate EVERY condition under which the round would stop honestly — then check the plan's kill rules actually TRIGGER on those conditions (walk each: a control reaching target — does the plan name WHO declares the stop and at what point? a seat dying mid-wave — the plan says no replacement; is the claim still verifiable with seat X dead? the erratum diff exceeding its declared bounds — is there a mechanical check? the S229 page having moved semantically — does any check notice?). For each gap: the missing sentence, written as a proposed plan amendment the L1 can adopt verbatim. Publish {W}/w1/Kb80C04Honesty.md.""")

add("Kb80C05Closure", "critic", f"""Simulate the non-author closure. Read {L}/corpus/lessons/round-79-verifier-spotcheck.md to learn the closure's method (step-1 conformance, per-claim re-check, discernment table, routed defects). Then BE the closure for round 80's design: re-derive the before-values independently (base battery, gate truths), check the freeze discipline (was anything scored before its counter froze? were the controls run before data?), and list the top-5 attacks the real closure will mount (with the exact commands it will run). Publish {W}/w1/Kb80C05Closure.md — this is the single most valuable document for the L1's integration pass.""")

add("Kb80V01C1", "reviewer", f"""Line-review the C1 stack. Read every C1 patch (E01–E06 outputs) line by line against {L}/plans/round-80.md § C1: does each patch do exactly what the plan's repair sentence says and nothing else? Check the diff scopes (no stray bytes, no unrelated whitespace), the SKILL.md table cell syntax against check_gate_registry.py's parser (run the parser on the patched file in scratch), the symlink mode (120000, relative, four hops), the refresh script's write set, and the erratum diff (exactly LINE_CAP + pins + in-step self-test arms). Any defect: file:line + severity + the minimal fix. Publish {W}/w1/Kb80V01C1.md with verdict CLEAN or DEFECTS(list).""")

add("Kb80V02C2", "reviewer", f"""Review C2 against the frozen predicate. Read {L}/plans/round-80.md § C2 and the E07/E08/E09 outputs; verify the counter implements each conjunct (a)–(i) exactly once and the self-test covers each with a red-before arm; verify the entry's quotes occur verbatim in their snapshots (re-run the occurrence checks yourself); verify the frozen surface table (header, first cells, grant labels); verify the exit predicate is N==6 and the three printed lines are exactly `allowed-tools surfaces documented N of 6`, `generator world: M of 6`, `semantic rider: ...`. Defects: file:line + fix. Publish {W}/w1/Kb80V02C2.md with verdict.""")

add("Kb80V03Instruments", "reviewer", f"""Pin/blob exactness audit. Independently recompute (from PRIMARY bytes only): the E04-patched verify_corpus_integrity.py git blob, the E05-patched verify_corpus_independent.py git blob, the amended check_tip_battery.py sha256, and each INSTRUMENT_PINS entry's coverage of the CURRENT {BASE} blobs for the four untouched instruments. Cross-check against E04/E05/E06's published values and {W}/build/Kb80E16PinAudit.txt. Also verify: the two edited instruments, at the stack world, still pass their OWN self-tests and the floors guard still passes them (floors rows corpus_integrity/independent are floor-semantics — confirm observed ≥ floor after the edits). Publish {W}/w1/Kb80V03Instruments.md with a table of every pin: value, recomputed, agrees(y/n).""")

add("Kb80V04Gates", "reviewer", f"""Independent gate-green audit. In a SECOND, independent scratch clone (build it yourself from {BASE}; do not reuse any sibling's clone), apply the full landed stack (gather the patches from {W}/build/; if any is missing, mark the corresponding gate unverifiable rather than borrowing another seat's world): registry, symlink, rows, E04, grammar, C2 files, amended counter. Run each battery gate yourself with direct exit capture: all six must exit 0; the two corpus gates' --json must show zero DRIFTs; the amended battery must print `guards 5 of 5; tip battery exit 0: 6 of 6` in BOTH forms. Publish {W}/w1/Kb80V04Gates.md with the receipts.""")

add("Kb80V05Report", "reviewer", f"""Evidence-pointer integrity review. Read the scribes' drafts (S01–S05, S08, S15) and check every path-like token resolves from the lane root at the stack world (build the resolution list yourself with a small python scanner for backticked `.ufo/`, `corpus/`, `verifiers/`, `plans/` tokens — or adapt {L}/verifiers/check_report_pointers.py's tokenizer read-only); flag every dead, schematic-unmarked or 4-hop token. Also verify the SHONEN box lines carry resolvable pointers and the Trend verdicts sections are present in the report skeleton. Publish {W}/w1/Kb80V05Report.md.""")

add("Kb80M01Instruments", "mutation-tester", f"""Mutation-test the instrument patches. Build mutants of the E04 and E05 patches (apply, then mutate the applied file, one mutation per world): drop the regression arm; flip the classification (external tokens back into unresolvable); neutralize the `<rev>:` fix (make it always-True); weaken the arm's assertion; pad the diff with comment lines to the cap boundary (60) and one past it. Each mutant: run the affected gate's self-test + the amended counter's guards at the mutant world — the self-test or a guard MUST fail; a mutant passing everything is a KILL (report with the exact mutant diff). Publish {W}/w1/Kb80M01Instruments.md with a mutant table (mutant, killed-by, receipt).""")

add("Kb80M02Battery", "mutation-tester", f"""Mutation-test the amended counter. Mutants of the E5 erratum: (1) pin table gains a random (unreviewed) blob; (2) LINE_CAP raised to 500; (3) BASE changed to the r80 base; (4) a guard's logic flipped (spotcheck-identity made to ignore deletions); (5) the W0 expectation string changed to `6 of 6`. Each mutant: run the counter's self-test and the W0 base world — each mutant must fail loudly; any that passes is a KILL. Publish {W}/w1/Kb80M02Battery.md with the mutant table.""")

add("Kb80M03C2", "mutation-tester", f"""Mutation-test the C2 counter. Mutants: exit predicate weakened to N≥5; the `generator world:` line dropped; the host check loosened to prefix-match; the quote check made substring-in-banner-allowed; the (b) used-by conjunct dropped; verified-at check loosened to any date. Each mutant: build the corresponding world (a real one — e.g. a sibling-host world for the prefix mutant) and show the mutant counter mis-scores it (exit 0 where the frozen counter exits 1) while the frozen counter kills it. A mutant indistinguishable from the frozen counter on your worlds is a KILL. Publish {W}/w1/Kb80M03C2.md.""")

add("Kb80M04Registry", "mutation-tester", f"""Mutation-test the SKILL.md/registry layer. Mutants of the E01 patch: a row with a wrong verdict map; a row missing the self-test flag; a `probe` kind instead of `gate`; the withdrawal annotation dropped; the third row naming a nonexistent script. Each mutant: run check_gate_registry.py in scratch — each must fail; then run the amended battery at the mutant world (registry red → battery < 6) to show the registry gate is load-bearing for C1. Publish {W}/w1/Kb80M04Registry.md.""")

add("Kb80Z01Sec", "reviewer", f"""Security review of the round-80 tooling. Read {W}/build/run-battery.sh, {W}/build/c2_controls.py, {R79W}/build/refresh-corpus-baseline.py, the E04/E05 instrument diffs, and the C2 counter: look for unsafe writes (path traversal via arguments, symlink following where a file is expected, writes outside the sandbox/--work), shell injection (unquoted variables in run-battery.sh), credential handling in fetch records, and network exfiltration paths. Also review the seat-prompt write-scope bounds (could a seat's instructions make it write outside {L}?). Findings: severity + file:line + minimal fix; no findings is a fine verdict if earned. Publish {W}/w1/Kb80Z01Sec.md.""")

add("Kb80N01Sentinel", "sentinel", f"""Source-stability sentinel. At your START, fetch the six C2 pages once (status/bytes/sha256/utc). At your END (after all other seats land), fetch again and diff: any page whose sha256 moved between your two fetches, or vs the committed snapshot, gets a variance report (S229's Akamai class is the known shape — three bot-manager lines). Also watch the four negative-sweep pages the NegSweep seat flags as unstable. Your verdict names every page STABLE or VARIED(bytes, class) — the L1 needs this to decide recheck cells. Publish {W}/w1/Kb80N01Sentinel.md.""")

add("Kb80Y01Simplify", "simplifier", f"""Minimize the C1 stack. Read the E01–E06 patches as they land: is any hunk removable without changing a gate verdict or breaking a plan conjunct? Candidates: SKILL.md row cells (shorten while keeping parser-conformance), the registry withdrawal annotations (required by Q46?), the grammar-class implementation (can it be 3 lines instead of 10 in either gate?), the erratum self-test arm updates. For each candidate: the removal diff, the re-run receipts proving gates still green, and a verdict REMOVABLE (with the smaller patch returned as {W}/build/Kb80Y01-simplified.patch) or REQUIRED (the conjunct/gate that breaks). Never simplify away a red-before arm. Publish {W}/w1/Kb80Y01Simplify.md.""")

add("Kb80G01Revenge", "revenger", f"""Re-derive the r79 rejection; verify r80 answers it. Read {L}/corpus/lessons/round-79.md § Mistakes (M79-1) and Q74, then the r79 control receipt {R79W}/build/Kb79X02C2Controls.receipts.txt. Independently reconstruct C4 (the mechanical generator that reached 6 of 6) at the r79 draft counter — confirm the r79 verdict yourself. Then check the r80 design against it: does the r80 claim REST on beating the generator (it must not), does the counter PRINT the generator's score (it must), is the semantic rider the only part that claims what the generator cannot reach (it must be), and are the reviewers of record non-author (the closure is; check the plan's exact wording makes them named, not generic). Verdict: ANSWERED or NOT-ANSWERED (with the gap). Publish {W}/w1/Kb80G01Revenge.md.""")

add("Kb80L01Grammar", "scout", f"""Probe the grammar class (feeds E05). Run a bounded probe of BOTH corpus gates' CURRENT ({BASE}) commit-token handling with ~20 synthetic shapes appended to a scratch copy of a corpus file: `owner/repo@<hex>` (the r80 tokens' shape), `owner/repo@<hex>` inside backticks, a bare `<hex>` lane-absent, a bare `<hex>` fleet-present, `HEAD:<path>`, `owner/repo@<short>` (7 hex), `<hex>@owner/repo` (reversed), a 40-hex sha256-looking token (is it mistaken for a commit?), `owner/repo@<hex>` inside a double-backtick span (the round-54 spotcheck's exact shape), `owner@<hex>` (no slash). For each shape: which class does each gate assign (unresolvable/external/nonhash/quoted/resolved)? One table, receipts. This map is E05's spec input — publish it EARLY at {W}/build/Kb80L01Grammar.md so E05 can read it.""")

add("Kb80L02Ctl", "critic", f"""Probe r79's shortcut worlds on the amended counter. Re-run the r79 world set that still applies under erratum E5: W1 baseline-rewrite-to-observed, W2 floors lowered, W4 a LOG erratum token that excuses the dead citation without a correction row, W5 placeholder registry rows, W6 an index row for a nonexistent file, W7 a spot-check edit removing the HEAD: pointer (expect the spotcheck-identity guard failure — receipt). For each: the world diff, the amended counter's output, own exit. A world reaching 6 of 6 is a KILL of C1. Publish {W}/w1/Kb80L02Ctl.md.""")

add("Kb80L03Symlink", "scout", f"""Probe the symlink worlds. Worlds on the amended counter: (1) the symlink present but the fleet tree ABSENT (run the battery with --fleet pointing at a fleet-less parent you construct — read the counter's --fleet handling) → the checklist gate must re-red (dangling = fails closed); (2) the symlink with THREE hops (wrong depth) → must not resolve; (3) a REGULAR FILE copy at the same path with identical bytes → checklist greens (existence test) — this is the known existence-vs-identity limit: verify the identity evidence (sha256 equality) is what the round's receipts carry, and that the report says so; (4) the symlink added AND LOG.md:2818 edited to a `../` form → the `..`-cap landmine must fire or the edit must be within 3 hops — which is it? Publish {W}/w1/Kb80L03Symlink.md with verdicts.""")

